  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/a-computational-social-science-approach-to-examine-the-duality-between-productivity-and-cybersecurit",
    "slug": "post__a-computational-social-science-approach-to-examine-the-duality-between-productivity-and-cybersecurit",
    "title": "A Computational Social Science Approach to Examine the Duality between Productivity and Cybersecurity Policy Compliance within Organizations",
    "description": "Employees often face a conflict between productivity and adhering to cybersecurity policies, which can lead to decreased performance and increased stress. The emphasis an organization places on productivity versus cybersecurity influences how employees balance these responsibilities, with decisions often driven by habitual behavior rather than rational cost-benefit analyses. This research explores how varying workloads, organizational culture, and the friction between compliance and productivity",
    "text_chars": 4814,
    "h1": "A Computational Social Science Approach to Examine the Duality between Productivity and Cybersecurity Policy Compliance within Organizations"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/coupons-as-cognitive-malware-attacking-interconnected-systems",
    "slug": "post__coupons-as-cognitive-malware-attacking-interconnected-systems",
    "title": "Coupons as Cognitive Malware: Attacking Interconnected Systems",
    "description": "Dr. Matthew Canham Imagine that you receive an email from your electricity provider offering a 50% discount on electricity for using power during certain times of the day. The message explains that rising demand for electricity costs caused by increased data center usage is stressing the power grid and the electricity provider is exploring ways to encourage users to increase usage during prescribed times to reduce straining the power grid. The message appears legitimate and there are no indicati",
    "text_chars": 22308,
    "h1": "Coupons as Cognitive Malware: Attacking Interconnected Systems"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/darwin-monkey-next-gen-neuromorphic-computing-and-competition-for-cognitive-capability-control",
    "slug": "post__darwin-monkey-next-gen-neuromorphic-computing-and-competition-for-cognitive-capability-control",
    "title": "Darwin Monkey: Next Generation Neuromorphic Computing and Competition for Cognitive Capability and Control",
    "description": "Elise AnnettJames Giordano\u00a0The Darwin Monkey\u00a0System: A Paradigm Shift from AI to Synthetic CognitionFully integrated neuromorphic computing represents an important \u2013 and provocative \u2013 developmental iteration of artificial intelligence (AI). To date, most AI system operations have been based upon symbolic reasoning and/or statistical inference derived from large data sets. However, Darwin Monkey, a large-scale neuromorphic computing system newly developed by Chinese researchers, was explicitly en",
    "text_chars": 20166,
    "h1": "Darwin Monkey: Next Generation Neuromorphic Computing and Competition for Cognitive Capability and Control"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/developing-a-neurosecurity-framework-to-defend-against-the-coming-neurowar",
    "slug": "post__developing-a-neurosecurity-framework-to-defend-against-the-coming-neurowar",
    "title": "Developing a Neurosecurity Framework to Defend Against the Coming Neurowar",
    "description": "Matthew CanhamThe capture of Nicol\u00e1s Maduro by US special operations forces without enduring the loss of a single operator marks one of the most overwhelmingly successful operations in the (acknowledged) history of special operations. A recent account by one of the Venezuelan security guards loyal to Maduro suggests that some advanced weaponry\u00a0may have been involved. Their account describes hearing a loud and intense sound which caused the guards to experience extreme pain in their heads and fal",
    "text_chars": 18313,
    "h1": "Developing a Neurosecurity Framework to Defend Against the Coming Neurowar"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/developing-training-research-to-improve-cyber-defense-of-industrial-control-systems",
    "slug": "post__developing-training-research-to-improve-cyber-defense-of-industrial-control-systems",
    "title": "Developing Training Research to Improve Cyber Defense of Industrial Control Systems",
    "description": "Cyber-attacks on Industrial Control Systems (ICS) can be particularly severe, as they may manipulate sensor and control data to cause significant damage, as exemplified by the Stuxnet worm. Given the advanced nature of these attacks, current automated detection systems are insufficient, necessitating continued human involvement in ICS protection. We propose several training-based solutions to enhance the defense of these systems by better preparing human operators to identify and respond to such",
    "text_chars": 4685,
    "h1": "Developing Training Research to Improve Cyber Defense of Industrial Control Systems"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/exploring-the-hybrid-space",
    "slug": "post__exploring-the-hybrid-space",
    "title": "Exploring the Hybrid Space",
    "description": "As cyberspace becomes integral to military operations, terms like cyber power and cyber deterrence highlight its growing importance. Future military personnel will face increased complexity in joint operations where cyberspace is a key enabler. This paper proposes the Hybrid Space framework to better understand the interplay between cyberspace and the physical domain, addressing cognitive challenges and informing the development of effective education and training programs.Authors: \u00d8yvind J\u00f8sok,",
    "text_chars": 4656,
    "h1": "Exploring the Hybrid Space"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/macrocognition-applied-to-the-hybrid-space-team-environment-functions-and-processes-in-cyber-opera",
    "slug": "post__macrocognition-applied-to-the-hybrid-space-team-environment-functions-and-processes-in-cyber-opera",
    "title": "Macrocognition Applied to the Hybrid Space: Team Environment, Functions and Processes in Cyber Operations",
    "description": "As military cyber operations become more integrated, effective team coordination is crucial. This paper expands on a macrocognitive model by introducing the Hybrid Space framework, which maps cognitive processes across cyber-physical and tactical-strategic dimensions. This framework aims to enhance understanding of environmental complexity and improve team performance in cyber contexts. The paper also discusses insights from cyber network defense exercises at the Norwegian Defence Cyber Academy",
    "text_chars": 4844,
    "h1": "Macrocognition Applied to the Hybrid Space: Team Environment, Functions and Processes in Cyber Operations"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/neurosecurity-human-brain-electro-optical-signals-as-masint",
    "slug": "post__neurosecurity-human-brain-electro-optical-signals-as-masint",
    "title": "Neurosecurity: Human Brain Electro-Optical Signals as MASINT",
    "description": "Advances in neuroimaging and data analysis are making it increasingly possible to probe and gather intelligence from individual minds without their cooperation. This shift suggests we are moving towards a reality where human thought may no longer be considered entirely private. The ability to read, write to, and analyze brain activity aligns with Measurement and Signals Intelligence (MASINT) practices, highlighting the need for discussions on the ethical and practical implications of neurosecuri",
    "text_chars": 4728,
    "h1": "Neurosecurity: Human Brain Electro-Optical Signals as MASINT"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/nist-special-publication-minding-the-gaps-in-human-centered-cybersecurity",
    "slug": "post__nist-special-publication-minding-the-gaps-in-human-centered-cybersecurity",
    "title": "NIST Special Publication: Minding the Gaps in Human-Centered Cybersecurity",
    "description": "In August 2024, the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) co-sponsored ConnectCon, an interactive workshop that facilitated meaningful conversations and connections between researchers and practitioners on the topic of human-centered cybersecurity. During the workshop, cybersecurity and human factors experts came to consensus on what they saw as the most pressing human-centered cybersecurity challenges today and potential solutions to address those challenges. This report provide",
    "text_chars": 4687,
    "h1": "NIST Special Publication: Minding the Gaps in Human-Centered Cybersecurity"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/socio-technical-communication-the-hybrid-space-and-the-olb-model-for-science-based-cyber-education",
    "slug": "post__socio-technical-communication-the-hybrid-space-and-the-olb-model-for-science-based-cyber-education",
    "title": "Socio-technical communication: The hybrid space and the OLB model for science-based cyber education",
    "description": "Lessons from safety-critical sociotechnical systems, such as aviation and acute medical care, underscore the vital role of human factors and effective communication between human agents. Failures in communication have been linked to many fatal incidents in aviation, highlighting the need for cognitive engineering to enhance performance in both civilian and military domains. The Hybrid Space framework provides a blueprint for addressing cognitive and behavioral challenges in military cyber operat",
    "text_chars": 5060,
    "h1": "Socio-technical communication: The hybrid space and the OLB model for science-based cyber education"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/the-enduring-mystery-of-the-repeat-clickers",
    "slug": "post__the-enduring-mystery-of-the-repeat-clickers",
    "title": "The Enduring Mystery of the Repeat Clickers",
    "description": "Repeat Clickers\u2014individuals within an organization who consistently fall for phishing emails\u2014pose a significant security risk. This behavior, affecting a persistent minority, may be driven by individual traits or an interaction between these traits and external factors such as culture or social engineering tactics. Understanding the causes behind repeat clicking is crucial for developing effective mitigations, which could significantly enhance organizational security. This paper presents pilot d",
    "text_chars": 4707,
    "h1": "The Enduring Mystery of the Repeat Clickers"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/understanding-online-information-operations-development-of-an-influence-network-for-scientific-inqu",
    "slug": "post__understanding-online-information-operations-development-of-an-influence-network-for-scientific-inqu",
    "title": "Understanding Online Information Operations: Development of an Influence Network for Scientific Inquiry Testing Environment (INSITE)",
    "description": "Influence operations that spread propaganda, disinformation, and social hysteria pose a critical threat to U.S. national security. Effective countermeasures must be developed to respond quickly and anticipate future threats. A major challenge in creating these countermeasures is the lack of a sophisticated testing environment for evaluating detection tools and algorithms. By integrating social sciences with applied mathematics, researchers can better understand social influence and response beha",
    "text_chars": 5093,
    "h1": "Understanding Online Information Operations: Development of an Influence Network for Scientific Inquiry Testing Environment (INSITE)"
  },
  {
    "url": "https://www.cognitivesecurityinstitute.org/post/what-can-cognitive-security-learn-from-the-b-17-flying-fortress",
    "slug": "post__what-can-cognitive-security-learn-from-the-b-17-flying-fortress",
    "title": "What Can Cognitive Security Learn From The B-17 Flying Fortress?",
    "description": "When are people the most vulnerable to a social engineering attack?\u00a0When are they most likely to make a mistake?\u00a0If we pause to contemplate these two questions, we will likely come to a very similar list of conclusions; when they are tired, distracted, or they confuse one thing for another.\u00a0In the first few years of World War II, the U.S. Army Air Corps lost over 400 aircraft in less than two years, not from enemy engagements, but due to \u201cpilot error\u201d. Pilot error included incidents such as land",
    "text_chars": 11508,
    "h1": "What Can Cognitive Security Learn From The B-17 Flying Fortress?"
  },
